+\subsection{Reader Roadmap}
+The paper now follows a deliberately simple chain. First, it defines the claim boundary and why a strict-open synthetic benchmark is useful when measured positive-class truth is unavailable. Second, it walks from scenario groups to generated radar/cue products, detector views, train/CV selection, and blind-holdout scoring. Third, it makes the signal model explicit: what is sampled, what is randomized, what is held fixed, what is denied to models, and what is only a diagnostic visualization. Fourth, it separates three levels of detectors: single-sensor branches, accepted human-engineered fusion, and EI sparse calibrated fusion. Fifth, it reports one primary KPI and several guardrails, then pushes implementation details, source-code listings, and sealed high-IP EI components to the one-column appendices.
+
+\begin{reviewbox}[title=Tier-1 review contract]
+Every result in the main paper must answer three reviewer questions: (i) \emph{what exactly was simulated?}, (ii) \emph{what could the detector see?}, and (iii) \emph{what was locked before holdout scoring?}  The paper therefore avoids unsupported sensor-parity language, keeps named-platform claims prohibited, and treats phase/family slices as diagnostics unless a multi-seed measured validation study is added.
+\end{reviewbox}
+
 \subsection{Core Experiment}
 The core experiment compares classical radar and cue detector branches, tabular and sequence ML baselines, an accepted human-engineered prior fusion baseline, and an EI sparse calibrated late-fusion candidate. Scenario groups are locked before phase expansion, train/CV rows are used for candidate discovery and calibration, and the blind holdout is scored once after selection lock. The benchmark output remains synthetic public-proxy evidence only.
