+\subsection{Positive Public-Proxy Scenario Details}
+The paper uses the phrase ``fixed-wing pusher-prop public proxy'' in the main text and reserves Iranian-drone wording for appendix source-card context. The model is deliberately broad: a delta/swept fixed wing, rear pusher propulsor, a 45--60 m/s speed envelope, broad aspect/RCS envelope, and pusher-prop cadence proxy. The radar consequence is a compact fixed-wing return with high-cadence propulsion modulation and phase-dependent kinematics. The prohibited inference is equally important: this is not a measured Shahed/Geran radar signature, not a payload or route model, not an evasion model, and not a fielded-sensor surrogate.
+
+\subsection{Bird, RC, and Non-Airborne Hard Negatives}
+The hard-negative family is intentionally wider than a drone-vs-bird toy benchmark. Bird families cover single birds, dense flocks, shorebirds/waders, gulls/terns, raptors/falcons, large birds, seabirds/cormorants, and seasonal migratory density. RC fixed-wing aircraft are separated from birds because they can overlap the positive public proxy in prop cadence, speed, and aspect even though they are benign. Weather cells, terrain glint, multipath ghosts, RFI bursts, wind turbines, ground vehicles, and clutter-only counterfactuals are included because low-FPR radar performance is often dominated by false-track pressure rather than positive separation.
+
